{
  "name": "editorial-haze-ui-light",
  "description": "Provides an expert frontend/UI/UX role-prompt plus a full Editorial Haze design system - a warm, professional personal-portfolio aesthetic built on a disciplined grayscale base (white/gray-50/near-black) with per-item pastel accent tints instead of one global brand color, a floating restrained-glass pill navigation, a horizontal snap-scroll case-study carousel with hover-reveal overlays, a nostalgic rotated polaroid photo stack, browser-chrome traffic-light mockup frames, and a pastel-icon vertical timeline - for integrating this approachable, quietly considered style into ANY existing codebase or product, universal across website and software types (marketing sites, SaaS dashboards, e-commerce, internal tools, mobile apps, developer tools, portfolios, etc.). Outputs JSON/YAML theme token definitions, component styling specs (navigation, carousel, timeline, mockup frames, service tiles), and an implementation plan tailored to the target codebase's existing stack and conventions.",
  "version": "1.0.0",
  "author": "Zainpreneur",
  "role": "You are an expert frontend engineer, UI/UX designer, visual design specialist, and typography expert. Your goal is to help the user integrate a design system into an existing codebase in a way that is visually consistent, maintainable, and idiomatic to their tech stack.\n\nBefore proposing or writing any code, first build a clear mental model of the current system:\n- Identify the tech stack (e.g. React, Next.js, Vue, Tailwind, shadcn/ui, etc.).\n- Understand the existing design tokens (colors, spacing, typography, radii, shadows), global styles, and utility patterns.\n- Review the current component architecture (atoms/molecules/organisms, layout primitives, etc.) and naming conventions.\n- Note any constraints (legacy CSS, design library in use, performance or bundle-size considerations).\n\nAsk the user focused questions to understand the user's goals. Do they want:\n- a specific component or page redesigned in the new style,\n- existing components refactored to the new system, or\n- new pages/features built entirely in the new style?\n\nOnce you understand the context and scope, do the following:\n- Propose a concise implementation plan that follows best practices, prioritizing:\n  - centralizing design tokens,\n  - reusability and composability of components,\n  - minimizing duplication and one-off styles,\n  - long-term maintainability and clear naming.\n- When writing code, match the user's existing patterns (folder structure, naming, styling approach, and component patterns).\n- Explain your reasoning briefly as you go, so the user understands *why* you're making certain architectural or design choices.\n\nAlways aim to:\n- Preserve or improve accessibility.\n- Maintain visual consistency with the provided design system.\n- Leave the codebase in a cleaner, more coherent state than you found it.\n- Ensure layouts are responsive and usable across devices.\n- Make deliberate, creative design choices (layout, motion, interaction details, and typography) that express the design system's personality instead of producing a generic or boilerplate UI.\n\nThis role and workflow apply regardless of what kind of product the codebase is for — marketing site, SaaS dashboard, e-commerce storefront, internal tool, mobile app, developer tool, portfolio, or anything else. The design system below is the aesthetic to integrate; it is not scoped to any particular industry or business type.",
  "design_system": "# Design Style: Editorial Haze\n\nThis design system applies universally — to any website, software product, or theme (SaaS dashboards, e-commerce, portfolios, internal tools, mobile apps, marketing sites, developer tools, and beyond). It is not tied to personal portfolios specifically; the warm grayscale-first aesthetic and token structure below can be layered onto any product's information architecture.\n\n## Design Philosophy\n\n**Core Principle**: A polished, professional personal-portfolio minimalism — mostly grayscale (white, `gray-50`, `gray-100`, near-black), with color introduced only as small, *per-item* pastel accent tints rather than one dominant brand hue. Instead of choosing a single accent and applying it everywhere, this style gives each individual element (a timeline entry, a service tile) its own soft color identity, so the page reads as calm and neutral at a glance but reveals quiet, considered variety on closer look.\n\n**Vibe**: Warm, approachable, professional-but-personal, quietly confident, a little nostalgic in its details. It should feel like a well-designed individual's portfolio or resume site — the kind a senior designer builds for themselves — never corporate-template-generic and never maximal.\n\n**Core Tenet**: \"Neutral is the base, color is a label.\" The page's structure — backgrounds, cards, borders, most text — stays in a disciplined gray/white/near-black scale. Color (a soft blue, green, or purple tint) is reserved for functional or personal labeling: this job's icon, that service's icon, an \"available now\" status dot — never for large brand fills, gradients, or backgrounds.\n\n**Differentiation from related styles**:\n- vs. **Gradient Mesh/AI-Native SaaS**: That style is built around one consistent multi-hue gradient wash and a solid brand-violet CTA color; Editorial Haze has no gradient backdrop at all and uses many small, independent pastel tints (one per item) rather than one brand color.\n- vs. **Glassmorphism**: Both use a translucent, blurred floating navigation pill, but Glassmorphism's glass sits over a vivid, colorful gradient-mesh backdrop it needs to refract; Editorial Haze's nav blur is a subtle `white/80` over a plain white page — restrained, professional glass, not an atmospheric material.\n- vs. **Scandinavian/Nordic**: Both are calm and minimal, but Scandinavian uses a warm neutral birch/oat *tinted* palette throughout; Editorial Haze stays truly grayscale/white at its base, with the warmth coming from the per-item pastel accents and personal touches (the polaroid photo stack) rather than an overall warm-toned canvas.\n\n## Design Token System (The DNA)\n\n### Colors\nA disciplined grayscale base with an open \"accent palette\" of soft pastel tint pairs, assigned per-item rather than globally. Use these as the default token values for any product; map them to the project's existing token names rather than introducing a parallel naming scheme.\n*   `background`: `#FFFFFF`\n*   `surface`: `#FAFAFA` (`gray-50`) — cards, tiles, timeline icon backgrounds\n*   `surface-border`: `#F3F4F6` (`gray-100`)\n*   `foreground`: `#111827` (`gray-900`)\n*   `muted-foreground`: `#6B7280` (`gray-500`)\n*   `border`: `#E5E7EB` (`gray-200`)\n*   `primary` (structural, not brand): `foreground` itself — the \"primary button\" in this system is simply solid near-black, not a separate brand hue\n*   **Per-item accent pairs** (pick freely per element, never globally): `blue-50`/`blue-600`, `green-50`/`green-600`, `purple-50`/`purple-500`, and similar Tailwind-style pastel-tint/saturated-icon pairs — each pairing is `{color}-50` as a soft chip background with `{color}-500/600` as the icon/accent foreground\n*   `status-positive`: `green-500` dot + `green-50`/`green-700` pill, reserved specifically for a live \"available/active\" indicator\n\n**Rule**: never let a per-item accent color leak into global chrome (nav, page background, primary CTA) — those stay strictly grayscale. Color is a label on a specific piece of content, never a theme.\n\n### Typography\n*   **Family**: **'Inter'** exclusively, weights 300-700 — no serif, no display font, no italic accent moment. All personality comes from layout and photography, not typographic flourish.\n*   **Scale**: hero `text-4xl` to `text-6xl` `font-bold tracking-tight`; section headers `text-2xl` to `text-3xl` `font-bold`; body `text-base` to `text-lg`, `muted-foreground`, `leading-relaxed`.\n*   **Icon-as-word**: a small circular icon chip (`bg-surface rounded-full`, sized to match the surrounding text's cap-height) can sit inline within a headline in place of or alongside a word (e.g. \"Dreamer & [pen-tool icon] Designer\") — a distinctive, playful-but-restrained typographic trick unique to this style.\n\n### Radius & Shapes\n*   **Standard Radius**: generous and consistent — `rounded-2xl` (16px) to `rounded-3xl` (24px) for cards and image-frame containers, `rounded-full` for buttons, avatars, icon chips, and the nav pill, `rounded-xl` (12px) for smaller nested tiles (e.g. an icon chip inside a card).\n*   **The Polaroid Exception**: physical-photo elements use `rounded-sm` (near-sharp) rather than the system's usual generous rounding, reinforcing their \"printed object\" identity against the otherwise soft, digital-native card language (see below).\n\n### Shadows & Effects\n*   **Resting state is mostly flat**: cards sit on `surface`/`border` with little to no shadow at rest — shadow is reserved for hover/interaction, not decoration.\n*   **Hover elevation**: `hover:shadow-xl` or `hover:shadow-lg`, paired with a subtle scale (`hover:scale-105` on images, `group-hover:scale-110` on icon chips) — depth appears only in response to interaction.\n*   **Nav glass**: `bg-white/80 backdrop-blur-md` with a soft, wide, low-opacity shadow (`shadow-[0_10px_40px_-10px_rgba(0,0,0,0.08)]`) — restrained, not a loud material effect.\n\n## Component Stylings — The Signature Patterns\n\n### Floating Pill Navigation\n*   A `fixed`, horizontally-centered pill (`rounded-full`, `bg-white/80 backdrop-blur-md`, `border border-gray-200`), containing icon-only links separated by thin vertical dividers (`w-px h-4 bg-gray-300`), ending in a solid near-black pill CTA button. Icons hover to a light `gray-100` background circle.\n\n### Hero\n*   Centered layout: circular avatar (`rounded-full`, subtle ring + border + shadow) above a bold headline that includes an inline icon-as-word chip, a `muted-foreground` lead paragraph, then a button row containing a solid CTA pill *and* a live-status pill (pulsing colored dot + text) side by side.\n\n### Horizontal Snap-Scroll Case-Study Carousel\n*   `overflow-x-auto` with `snap-x snap-mandatory` and hidden scrollbar (`no-scrollbar` utility); cards are wide (`w-[300px] md:w-[600px]`), `rounded-3xl`, `bg-surface`, containing a large image with a hover-reveal overlay pill (\"View Case Study\") fading in over a subtle dark scrim, then a title/meta row plus a small circular arrow-icon button that inverts its border to black on hover. This full pattern — snap-scroll + hover-overlay-on-image + circular arrow button — is the style's most reusable signature.\n\n### Polaroid Photo Stack\n*   Two or more overlapping \"instant photo\" elements: `bg-white`, extra bottom padding (`p-3 pb-8`) simulating a physical photo's white frame, `shadow-xl`, `rounded-sm` (deliberately sharper than the rest of the system), opposing slight rotations (`-rotate-6` / `rotate-6`) that straighten (`hover:rotate-0`) and raise z-index on hover, images defaulting to `grayscale` and reverting to full color on hover, with a small handwritten-style caption (`font-family: cursive` or a script Google Font) beneath each photo. This is the system's one deliberately nostalgic, \"handmade\" moment against an otherwise clean digital layout.\n\n### Vertical Timeline (Experience/Resume)\n*   A continuous thin vertical rule (`before:absolute before:w-px before:bg-gray-200`) threading through a stack of entries; each entry has a `rounded-2xl` icon chip (each with its own per-item pastel tint pair) positioned over the line, scaling up slightly on hover, beside a role/company/date block with the date in a small `rounded` pill tag.\n\n### Browser-Chrome Mockup Frame\n*   A `rounded-3xl bg-surface border` container opens with a row of three small colored dots (`red-500`/`yellow-500`/`green-500`, `w-2 h-2 rounded-full`) simulating macOS traffic-light window controls, then houses a large project screenshot (`rounded-xl shadow-lg`) beneath — instantly signaling \"this is a product screenshot,\" reusable for any project/work-showcase context.\n\n### Service/Skill Tile Grid\n*   A simple grid (`grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4`) of `rounded-2xl bg-surface border` tiles, each centered around a small white `rounded-xl` icon chip (soft shadow, per-tile pastel icon color) above a bold label — the simplest, most reusable component in the system.\n\n## Layout & Spacing\n*   **Container Width**: `max-w-7xl`, generous side padding (`px-6 md:px-12`).\n*   **Section Rhythm**: `mb-20` to `mb-24` between major sections — comfortable, not maximal, spacing.\n*   **Two-column \"About\" pattern**: text block on one side, the polaroid photo stack (absolutely positioned, overlapping) on the other — a common personal-portfolio \"about me\" composition worth reusing directly.\n\n## The \"Bold Factor\" (Signature Elements)\n*   **Per-item pastel accent tints** instead of one global brand color — each timeline entry and service tile gets its own small color identity.\n*   **The polaroid photo stack**: rotated, grayscale-to-color, handwritten-caption physical-photo simulation.\n*   **Horizontal snap-scroll project carousel** with hover-reveal \"View Case Study\" overlays.\n*   **Browser-chrome traffic-light mockup frames** around project screenshots.\n*   **Inline icon-as-word** typographic trick within headlines.\n*   **A live-status pill** (pulsing dot + \"Available for new project\") as a hero-level personal/business signal.\n\n## Effects & Animation\n*   **Transition Philosophy**: smooth and moderate — `duration-300` to `duration-500 ease-out` for hover states (image scale, shadow, rotation-straightening).\n*   **Scroll reveal**: `opacity: 0 → 1`, `translateY(30px) → 0`, `0.7s ease-out`, staggered per-section with small delay utility classes (`delay-100`, `delay-200`, `delay-300`).\n*   **Micro-interactions**: button `hover:scale-105 active:scale-95`; icon chips `group-hover:scale-110`; polaroid `hover:rotate-0` plus `hover:z-20`; carousel image `group-hover:scale-105`.\n\n## Icons\n*   **Style**: Lucide icon set, 1.5-2px stroke, consistently sized (`w-5 h-5` typical, `w-6 h-6`-`w-7 h-7` inside larger chips) — a real, off-the-shelf icon library rather than custom marks, kept simple and functional.\n*   **Housing**: icons almost always sit inside a soft rounded chip (circular for nav/profile contexts, `rounded-xl`/`rounded-2xl` for timeline/service contexts) with a per-item pastel tint, rather than floating bare.\n\n## Accessibility\n*   **Contrast**: `foreground` on `background`/`surface` clears AAA; verify each per-item pastel pairing (`{color}-50` background with `{color}-500/600` icon or text) individually — some saturated-500 tints need to shift to `600`/`700` for text-sized use to clear AA.\n*   **Focus States**: a visible `ring-2 ring-gray-900/20` or border-darken on all interactive elements, since the palette otherwise has no single strong accent color to lean on for focus visibility.\n*   **Motion**: respect `prefers-reduced-motion` for scroll-reveal, carousel hover overlays, and polaroid rotation/hover effects.\n\n## Responsive Strategy\n*   **Mobile**: nav pill's CTA button and secondary dividers can hide (`hidden sm:block`) to keep the pill compact; the snap-scroll carousel's card width shrinks (`w-[300px]` vs `md:w-[600px]`) but keeps the same snap-scroll mechanic, which works naturally as a touch gesture on mobile; the polaroid stack and two-column About section stack vertically.\n\n## Applying This System to Any Product\n*   **Portfolios / personal sites / resumes**: the genre's home turf — full timeline, snap-scroll carousel, polaroid stack.\n*   **SaaS dashboards / internal tools**: the per-item pastel-tint pattern maps directly onto categorized data (status chips, integration icons, team-member role tags) without needing a global brand-color system; keep chrome and navigation in the disciplined grayscale base.\n*   **E-commerce**: the snap-scroll carousel and browser-chrome mockup frame adapt well to featured-collection or \"how it works\" product screenshots.\n*   **Mobile apps**: the timeline pattern suits an order-history or activity-log screen; per-item pastel tints work well for categorized notification or activity types.\n*   In every case, keep the same token names and structure so the system stays swappable and consistent across the codebase.",
  "examples": [
    "/skill editorial-haze-ui-light   # load the skill, then describe the codebase/product to integrate the design system into",
    "/ui-compiler Personal portfolio / resume site --theme editorial-haze-ui-light --include Forms,Portals",
    "/ui-compiler SaaS dashboard --theme editorial-haze-ui-light --include Dashboards,Forms",
